<section id="section-00">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">00</span>
    <h2>Design Values</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-intro">Four core values underpin every decision in this data design language. They apply equally to visual charts, tables, written data summaries, and any other form of data presentation across digital and print channels.</p>
  <div class="grid-2 mb-lg">
    <div class="card-lg ">
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">01 — Clear</h3>
      <p class="text-body-sm">Data presentations are tailored to the information need. They are understandable and approachable for any reader in any context. Clarity is a discipline, not a default.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg ">
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">02 — Transparent</h3>
      <p class="text-body-sm">Visualisations reveal uncertainty, precision, data provenance, and coverage. Estimates, projections, and provisional figures are always distinguished from confirmed data. The reader should never be left guessing how confident to be in a number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg ">
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">03 — Open</h3>
      <p class="text-body-sm">Rich data experiences are created for everyone through accessible, inclusive, and adaptable approaches. Accessibility is a design constraint applied from the first decision — chart type, colour, label, interaction — not a final checklist item.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg ">
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">04 — Robust</h3>
      <p class="text-body-sm">Data can be consumed across web, print, presentation, social, and auditory outputs through resilient, lean solutions. No chart should depend on a single context to be legible. Design degrades gracefully across channels and screen sizes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card card--dark row-wrap">
    <div style="font-family:var(--font-display); font-weight:800; font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--brand-yellow); flex:0 0 auto; letter-spacing:0.03em;">HOW THESE VALUES INTERACT</div>
    <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:#ABABAB; line-height:1.6; flex:1; margin:0;">Clarity without transparency risks misleading. Transparency without openness excludes. Openness without robustness fails at scale. All four values must be satisfied simultaneously — a chart that is clear but inaccessible, or transparent but fragile across channels, does not meet this standard.</p>
  </div>
</section>
